From a615690818e395a62c354c4280d27dcdff9ed38f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Thu, 27 Aug 2026 13:29:18 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 10111/11362] Narrow the conductor's commit by CLAIM, not by
 pathspec

The conductor stages everything before committing, deliberately: it preserves
in-flight work rather than losing it, which is the standing commit-first
discipline. But it also sweeps other sessions' uncommitted work into
conductor-attributed commits. That happened four times on 2026-08-27, once
taking a guard while that guard was being written, and it produced a false
"your work was destroyed" report from an agent that then offered to rewrite
work which had in fact survived.

A fixed pathspec would be the dangerous fix. Agent-authored files outside it --
new tests, spec edits, experiment modules -- would be committed by nobody and
left exposed to the next checkout. So this still stages EVERYTHING first, then
unstages only paths another session has explicitly declared through
scripts/harness_integrity_lint.py. A declared path has an owner who is going to
commit it; an undeclared path is swept exactly as before. Nothing is stranded.

Safety valve: if the exclusions would leave nothing staged, they are abandoned
and the full sweep proceeds, with a warning naming the claimant. A session that
declares --scope '*' must not be able to turn a checkpoint into an empty
commit. Fail-open throughout, for the same reason
_restore_dropped_determinations is: any error leaves the old behaviour intact,
because committing work matters more than clean attribution.

Verified live on the real repo before wiring: a claimed probe was left unstaged
for its owner and logged by name, an unclaimed probe was staged as before, and
a peer claiming '*' triggered the valve rather than emptying a 24-file commit.

The decision rule is the pure function claimed_by_other_sessions, tested
without a git fixture per the precedent of determination_damage: 8 tests, and
3 of 3 mutations RED with the module restored byte-identical.

Applies at all five staging sites, including the two checkpoint paths that
caused the incident.

Two test-suite mutation markers were deleted deliberately rather than reverted.
Both flagged only tests/python/test_conductor_scope_narrowing.py -- the new
file added by this commit, an intended addition rather than test damage -- and
three ops/.*_state.json bookkeeping files carrying one-line timestamp changes.
Every path was marked [NOT this run] by the markers' own attribution. Nothing
under results/ or openspec/ was implicated, and no git checkout was performed:
reverting the reported list is how that guard destroyed authored work three
times.

REQ-INFRA-6800.

